    {
      "claim": "Imidafenacin, a basic heterocyclic amide with a primary amide, has 57.8% human absolute oral bioavailability, showing that the query's basic piperidine and primary amide do not preclude F >= 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C02", "C14", "C20", "C26", "C27", "C34", "C36", "C55", "C85"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 record C180 shows PNU96391, a methylsulfonyl-substituted piperidine analog, has near-complete GI absorption exceeding 90% in rats, supporting absorption for sulfonyl-containing basic amines.",
      "card_ids": ["C180"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 record C212 reports fluphenazine, the closest phenothiazine analog, as a P-gp substrate with an efflux ratio of 2.30; this is mechanistic evidence of possible gut-wall efflux but is not a direct oral-bioavailability outcome and is modest in magnitude.",
      "card_ids": ["C212"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 also contains examples of P-gp substrates with adequate absorption, including asimadoline with at least 20% absorbed within 1 h and esaxerenone with high Fa despite P-gp/BCRP substrate status, so efflux substrate status alone does not establish F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C223", "C210"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No L5 record provides affirmative threshold-relevant evidence for F < 20% for the query or a close analog; mechanism alone is insufficient to predict low oral bioavailability.",
      "card_ids": ["C212"]
    }
  ],
